### What are List.of, Set.of, and Map.of?

Java 9 introduced factory methods on the List, Set, and Map interfaces for creating immutable collections in a single expression. These collections reject all mutation attempts and do not allow null elements.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Set;

public class ImmutableFactoryDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001");
        Set<String> activeStatuses = Set.of("ACTIVE", "VERIFIED", "APPROVED");
        Map<String, String> owners = Map.of(
            "SAV2001", "Rajesh",
            "CUR3001", "Priya"
        );

        System.out.println("Accounts: " + accounts);
        System.out.println("Statuses: " + activeStatuses);
        System.out.println("Owners: " + owners);

        // All modification attempts throw UnsupportedOperationException
        try {
            accounts.add("NEW-001");
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.println("add() blocked: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }

        try {
            accounts.set(0, "CHANGED");
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.println("set() blocked: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Accounts: [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001]
Statuses: [ACTIVE, VERIFIED, APPROVED]
Owners: {CUR3001=Priya, SAV2001=Rajesh}
add() blocked: UnsupportedOperationException
set() blocked: UnsupportedOperationException
```

### Why do we need immutable collections?

Immutable collections solve three problems. First, they prevent accidental modification of shared data, which is especially important in multi-threaded banking applications. Second, they can be safely passed between methods without defensive copying. Third, they have compact internal representations that use less memory.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.List;

public class SafeSharingDemo {
    // This method returns an immutable list: callers cannot modify internal state
    public static List<String> getVipAccounts() {
        return List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> vips = getVipAccounts();
        System.out.println("VIP accounts: " + vips);

        try {
            vips.add("HACK-001"); // caller cannot tamper with the list
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.println("Cannot modify: safe!");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
VIP accounts: [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001]
Cannot modify: safe!
```

### How is Collections.unmodifiableList different from List.of?

Collections.unmodifiableList creates a read-only view that wraps the original mutable list. The view rejects modification, but if someone holds a reference to the original, they can still change it and the view reflects those changes.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class UnmodifiableVsImmutableDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // unmodifiableList: VIEW, not truly immutable
        List<String> original = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001"));
        List<String> view = Collections.unmodifiableList(original);

        System.out.println("View before: " + view);
        original.add("FD4001"); // modify the ORIGINAL
        System.out.println("View after: " + view); // view reflects the change!

        // List.of: truly immutable, no underlying mutable reference
        List<String> immutable = List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001");
        System.out.println("Immutable: " + immutable);
        // No way to modify this list from any reference
    }
}
```

```text
View before: [SAV2001, CUR3001]
View after: [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001]
Immutable: [SAV2001, CUR3001]
```

| Feature | List.of() | Collections.unmodifiableList() | List.copyOf() |
|---|---|---|---|
| Truly immutable | Yes | No (view) | Yes |
| Null elements | Throws NPE | Allows | Throws NPE |
| Backed by original | No | Yes | No |
| Memory | Compact | Wrapper + original | Compact |
| Since | Java 9 | Java 2 | Java 10 |

### How does List.copyOf work?

List.copyOf (Java 10) creates an immutable defensive copy of any collection. If the source is already an immutable List.of() result, copyOf returns the same instance (zero-copy optimisation).

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class CopyOfDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> mutable = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001"));
        List<String> copy = List.copyOf(mutable);

        mutable.add("FD4001"); // modify the original
        System.out.println("Original: " + mutable);
        System.out.println("Copy: " + copy); // copy is unaffected

        // Optimisation: copyOf an already immutable list returns same instance
        List<String> immutable = List.of("A", "B");
        List<String> copy2 = List.copyOf(immutable);
        System.out.println("Same instance: " + (immutable == copy2));
    }
}
```

```text
Original: [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001]
Copy: [SAV2001, CUR3001]
Same instance: true
```

### What are the rules and constraints?

- **No nulls.** `List.of(null)`, `Set.of(null)`, `Map.of(null, val)` all throw NullPointerException.
- **No duplicates in Set.of.** `Set.of("A", "A")` throws IllegalArgumentException.
- **No duplicate keys in Map.of.** `Map.of("k", "v1", "k", "v2")` throws IllegalArgumentException.
- **Map.of limit.** Map.of() supports up to 10 key-value pairs. For more, use Map.ofEntries.
- **Iteration order.** Set.of() and Map.of() do not guarantee iteration order. List.of() preserves insertion order.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Set;

public class ImmutableConstraintsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Null rejection
        try { List.of((String) null); }
        catch (NullPointerException e) { System.out.println("List.of(null): NPE"); }

        // Duplicate rejection in Set
        try { Set.of("SAV2001", "SAV2001"); }
        catch (IllegalArgumentException e) { System.out.println("Set.of duplicates: IAE"); }

        // Map.ofEntries for more than 10 pairs
        Map<String, String> bigMap = Map.ofEntries(
            Map.entry("SAV2001", "Rajesh"),
            Map.entry("CUR3001", "Priya"),
            Map.entry("FD4001", "Pankaj")
        );
        System.out.println("Map.ofEntries: " + bigMap);
    }
}
```

```text
List.of(null): NPE
Set.of duplicates: IAE
Map.ofEntries: {FD4001=Pankaj, SAV2001=Rajesh, CUR3001=Priya}
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Assuming unmodifiableList is truly immutable

```java
// WRONG: the caller can still modify through the original reference
List<String> original = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001"));
List<String> safe = Collections.unmodifiableList(original);
original.add("SNEAKY"); // modifies the "safe" list through the back door!
System.out.println(safe); // [SAV2001, SNEAKY]
```

```java
// CORRECT: use List.copyOf for a true defensive copy
List<String> original = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001"));
List<String> safe = List.copyOf(original);
original.add("SNEAKY");
System.out.println(safe); // [SAV2001] - unaffected
```

#### Mistake: Passing null to List.of

```java
// WRONG: List.of does not allow null
String name = null;
List<String> list = List.of(name); // NullPointerException!
```

```java
// CORRECT: filter nulls or use ArrayList if nulls are needed
import java.util.stream.*;
List<String> list = Stream.of("SAV2001", null, "CUR3001")
    .filter(java.util.Objects::nonNull)
    .toList();
```

### What about thread safety?

Immutable collections are inherently thread-safe for reads because their state never changes. No synchronisation is needed when sharing them across threads. This makes them ideal for configuration data, constant lists, and read-only reference data in concurrent banking applications.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Internal classes.** List.of() with 1-2 elements returns List12 (fields-based, no array). With 3+ elements, it returns ListN (compact array). This is more memory-efficient than ArrayList with its resizable array and size field.
- **Serialization.** Immutable collections are serializable. They deserialize to immutable instances.
- **toUnmodifiableList.** Java 10 added `Collectors.toUnmodifiableList()` and `Stream.toList()` (Java 16) that produce immutable lists directly from streams.
- **Shallow immutability.** List.of(account1, account2) is immutable at the list level, but the Account objects inside can still be mutated. For deep immutability, use records or ensure the elements are also immutable.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the difference between List.of and Collections.unmodifiableList?" : List.of is truly immutable with no backing mutable collection; unmodifiableList is a view that reflects changes to the original.
- "Can List.of contain null?" : No, it throws NullPointerException.
- "What happens if you pass duplicates to Set.of?" : IllegalArgumentException at creation time.
- "Is List.of thread-safe?" : Yes, because immutable collections cannot change state.
